>
> Usually I work with the method of Preacher&Hayes
>

In various papers, Preacher and Hayes frequently recommend bootstrapping for hypothesis tests involving indirect effects, since product terms are usually skewed.

>
>
> Please can you help me to interpret this output.

The bottom line of the output from parameterEstimates(fit, boot.ci.type = "perc") is the indirect effect a*b*c.  The point estimate is 0.109, with a SE = 0.046, which gives a significant Wald z test, z = 2.346, p = 0.019, so you can reject the null that this indirect effect is zero in the population.  You can also test the same null hypothesized population value of 0 (or any other null hypothesized population value you choose) by checking whether that number (i.e., 0) is within the confidence bounds: 95% CI = [ci.lower = 0.036, ci.upper = 0.216].  This interval does not contain zero, so you can reject that null hypothesized value and conclude that in the population, there is a positive indirect effect through this chain of 4 variables.

Terry

