Nope you didn't do anything wrong. By default cfa.mi will display a chi square statistic similar to the one computed by Mplus (which is almost identical to the MR chi square that is also available). The documentation for runMI (or cfa.mi) contains more information about the chi square pooling options. We provide several options in runMI but there currently isn't great guidance on which to use with nested model tests (or even without). We're working on some of these issues but they remain open. Honestly we probably need better documentation on the different chi square pooling options available.

